WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.050
- [Narrator] A solar eclipse happens

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:06.770
when a new moon moves between
the Earth and the sun,

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:09.220
blocking some or all of the sun's rays

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.473
from reaching the Earth.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.960
By cosmic chance, even though the sun

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.370
is 400 times wider than the moon,

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:19.310
it's also 400 times farther away.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:23.503
Therefore, the two objects
appear the same size in our sky.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.470
Astronomers are able to predict eclipses

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.053
because the Earth and moon
have very predictable orbits.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.463
Why, then, isn't there
an eclipse every month?

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.780
The moon's orbit is usually tilted

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.023
a few degrees north or south
in relation to the Earth.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.160
When the moon does eclipse the sun,

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.240
it casts two types of shadows on Earth:

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.203
a smaller, darker shadow,
known as the umbra,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.823
and a larger shadow,
known as the penumbra.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.790
There are four types of solar eclipses.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:04.270
The first and most spectacular
is a total eclipse,

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.960
when the moon completely
covers the sun's surface.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.900
A total eclipse can only be seen

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.830
if you're standing
within the umbral shadow.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:16.260
That's why the imaginary
line created by this shadow

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.980
as it races across Earth is
known as the path of totality.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.220
People within the penumbral shadow

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.310
see only a partial
eclipse, the second type.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.450
From this view, outside
the path of totality,

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.450
the moon passes in front
of the sun off-center,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.543
never fully covering its surface.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.440
Third, an annular eclipse,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.900
occurs when the moon passes
directly in front of the sun.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.420
However, unlike a total eclipse,

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.270
the moon appears too small
to fully cover the sun.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.050
The moon's orbit is elliptical,

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.040
so sometimes it's closer to Earth

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.843
and sometimes it's farther away.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.720
Last, a hybrid eclipse, is
when the moon's position

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.440
between the Earth and
sun is so finely balanced

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.340
that the curvature of
the Earth plays a role.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.100
The moon will be farther
away from some parts of Earth

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.500
along the eclipse's path,
resulting in an annular eclipse.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.380
In other parts, the moon
will be just close enough

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.763
to fully cover the sun,
resulting in a total eclipse.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.470
While a total solar eclipse happens

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.590
somewhere on Earth every year or two,

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.530
any given point on Earth
experiences the event

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.683
only about once every 400 years.

00:02:31.666 --> 00:02:32.499
(high-pitched hum)
(static crackles)

00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:33.530
We interrupt this video

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.550
for an important safety announcement.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.290
Looking directly at the sun,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.220
even during an eclipse, can
cause permanent eye damage.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.730
Fortunately, there are ways
to enjoy an eclipse safely.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.230
The easiest is to use certified
eclipse-watching glasses,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.490
which are shaded thousands of times darker

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.240
than typical sunglasses.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.440
You can also look at
the eclipse indirectly

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.360
by making a pinhole viewer.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.630
Simply poke a small hole
in a piece of cardboard.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.500
Hold the cardboard up to the sun,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.570
allowing the sun's image to be projected

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.300
onto a flat surface.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.550
Be sure to look only at the surface

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.203
and not through the cardboard.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.850
Just before the moon
completely covers the sun,

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.240
low-lying valleys on the moon's
edge will be the only spots

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.760
that sunlight continues to pass through.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.260
These remaining brilliant shafts of light,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.560
known as Baily's beads, will
disappear one after another.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.640
And finally, a single
bead of light remains,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.360
known as the Diamond Ring,

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.210
signaling that you're just seconds away

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.480
from experiencing totality.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.550
Once the last bead disappears

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.920
and the moon completely
covers the sun's surface,

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.240
the view through your eclipse
glasses will be pitch black.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:44.803
Totality achieved.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.850
At this point, none of the sun's
rays are reaching your eyes

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.810
and it's the only time that it's safe

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:55.120
to take off your glasses.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.580
Remember to put your
eclipse glasses back on

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:01.320
before any of the sun's rays
start to peek through again.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.390
Any sunlight reaching your
eyes, even for a few seconds,

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.420
can cause serious damage.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.030
While an eclipse can last a few hours,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.883
totality typically occurs
for less than three minutes.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:16.370
Animals and plants have also been known

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:19.690
to alter their behavior
during a total eclipse.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.450
Songbirds may stop singing,
crickets may start chirping,

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:27.000
and flowers might even start to close up.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.800
We won't always be able to
see total solar eclipses.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.610
The moon moves about one
and a half inches away

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.420
from Earth each year.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.060
It's estimated that in
about a billion years,

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.120
the moon will be too far away from Earth

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.783
to completely cover the sun.

00:04:44.822 --> 00:04:47.489
(elegant music)

